---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
const updated = "November 14, 2026";
---
<MainLayout title="Why West Covina's High-Risk Audit Findings Still Matter | StayJustIn" description="The State Auditor said West Covina still faced financial risks. A later City draft changed one audit rating. Here's what the documents show." canonical="https://www.stayjustin.com/accountability/west-covina-high-risk-audit/">
  <section class="article-page">
    <div class="container article-container">
      <p class="page-kicker">ACCOUNTABILITY · UPDATED {updated}</p>
      <h1>City Says a Contract Oversight Problem Is Fixed. Has the State Auditor Agreed?</h1>
      <p class="article-dek">West Covina's 2024 state audit found three unresolved risk areas. A City draft from August 2026 lists only two. The difference raises a straightforward question: what evidence supports the City's new rating?</p>
      <p><strong>What we know:</strong> The California State Auditor's December 2024 report rated contract oversight as <strong>partially addressed</strong>. West Covina's August 2026 draft corrective action plan rates it <strong>fully addressed</strong>. The documents reviewed do not include a subsequent State Auditor determination accepting that change.</p>
      <h2>What changed between the reports?</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto"><table><thead><tr><th>Risk area</th><th>State Auditor · Dec. 2024</th><th>City draft · Aug. 2026</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>General Fund reserves</td><td>Partially addressed</td><td>Partially addressed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use of City resources and health benefits</td><td>Partially addressed</td><td>Partially addressed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Financial decision analysis</td><td>Fully addressed</td><td>Fully addressed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Financial recovery plan</td><td>Fully addressed</td><td>Fully addressed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purchase card management</td><td>Fully addressed</td><td>Fully addressed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Contract oversight and best value</td><td><strong>Partially addressed</strong></td><td><strong>Fully addressed</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>This difference is documented. It does not, on its own, prove the City misrepresented its progress. The City may be relying on policy changes after the auditor's review. The outstanding question is whether those changes were evaluated and accepted by the State Auditor.</p>
      <h2>There is evidence of a policy improvement</h2>
      <p>West Covina Municipal Code § 2-330, amended December 3, 2024, requires the City to document why agreements exceeding five years are in its best interest. The State Auditor had previously identified the lack of such a requirement. A written policy is a meaningful change, but the records reviewed do not independently establish how consistently it is being followed.</p>
      <h2>The reserve cushion remains a concern</h2>
      <p>The State Auditor reported that General Fund reserves grew from $12.6 million in fiscal year 2019–20 to $17.7 million in fiscal year 2022–23. It also found operating revenue exceeded operating expenses, an improvement from the earlier structural deficit. Nonetheless, the auditor recommended a reserve target above the City's then-current 17% minimum because of substantial pension-related obligations.</p>
      <p>The City's draft says the Council raised its minimum unassigned General Fund balance requirement from 17% to 18% on February 18, 2025. Whether that one-percentage-point increase adequately addresses the auditor's concern has not been established by the documents reviewed.</p>
      <h2>Employee health benefits: still unresolved in the City's draft</h2>
      <p>The State Auditor found that West Covina paid 95% of employee healthcare premiums as of fiscal year 2022–23. The August 2026 draft says that, as of November 2025, all nine employee unions had agreements with no changes to medical benefit levels. The draft promises continued good-faith efforts in future bargaining; it does not document a reduction in those costs.</p>
      <h2>Questions residents deserve answers to</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Did the California State Auditor formally change its assessment of contract oversight from partially addressed to fully addressed? If so, when and based on what evidence?</li>
        <li>What records show that the City's five-year contract documentation requirement is being applied in practice?</li>
        <li>How was the new 18% reserve minimum evaluated against pension-related debt and other financial risks?</li>
        <li>What has changed in employee medical benefit contributions since November 2025?</li>
        <li>What corrective-action milestones remain open, and when will the public receive the next progress report?</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Sources and reporting limits</h2>
      <p>Based on California State Auditor Report 2024-801 (December 19, 2024), the City's August 2026 <em>draft</em> corrective action plan, and West Covina Municipal Code Article VII, including § 2-330. This is an analysis of those records, not an independent confirmation of the City's audit status in November 2026. No finding of fraud, insolvency, or deliberate misrepresentation is supported by these documents.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.auditor.ca.gov/reports/2024-801/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the California State Auditor's source report</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
